Transport connects the source to the sensitive place (@cm-transport-entry: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../sources/Contamination-Control-Engineering-NASA.lingua#r_2XKKPYTW2DPQPQZ8VPV23BJ8A5", #source "../sources/Air-Cleaning-Handbook-DOE.lingua#r_09QPEZ76E9W6HS7R55X2K8TB3X", #clean-manufacturing "Flux-deposition-and-time-of-exposure.lingua#r_3ZWNG6Y02XA6FZ3V8AVB6BCZJP") { r_79HSQ4MDC0MQ3Z2GJMR6133HXG
**Read:** [Contamination control engineering — Tribble and colleagues, NASA CR 4740](../sources/Contamination-Control-Engineering-NASA.lingua#r_2XKKPYTW2DPQPQZ8VPV23BJ8A5), prevention and monitoring; [Air-cleaning systems — DOE-HDBK-1169-2022](../sources/Air-Cleaning-Handbook-DOE.lingua#r_09QPEZ76E9W6HS7R55X2K8TB3X), aerosol behavior and filter collection.

Identify a source, a route of transfer and a sensitive destination. Airflow, liquid flow, contact, gravity, random molecular motion and electrical forces can contribute differently. Removing one route does not establish that the others are negligible.

Choose the tracked quantity: number in a size interval, mass of a chemical species or a biological observation. Write its accumulation as input minus output plus generation minus removal or transformation. Keep a deposited surface inventory separate from the airborne one; material can move between them.

**Work:** Draw the paths from a moving part, a person and a cleaning operation to one sensitive surface. For each, identify a measurable source quantity, a plausible transfer mechanism and an observation that could distinguish that mechanism from another.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
